<Layout pageTitle="Interface design guide" subtitle="Evidence, routing reasoning and fabrication assumptions." description="Primary literature and engineering reasoning behind the Neural Interface Designer’s electrode shapes, lead arrangements, flexible cables and photolithography concept layers.">
  <article class="guide">
    <a class="back" href="/simulations/interface-designer/">← Open the neural interface designer</a>
    <p class="intro">The designer is a micrometer-scale layout sandbox for planar thin-film interfaces. It connects each contact to an individual pad, separates metal planes and makes incomplete routing visible. Geometry is the foundation for later electrical and mechanical simulations, rather than a prediction of implant performance.</p>
    <nav class="contents" aria-label="Guide contents"><a href="#workflow">Getting started</a><a href="#contacts">Contacts & arrays</a><a href="#routing">Lead arrangements</a><a href="#mechanics">Flexible leads</a><a href="#fabrication">Photolithography</a><a href="#exports">Export conventions</a><a href="#sources">Primary sources</a></nav>
    <section id="workflow"><h2>Start with a question you can compare</h2><ol><li>Generate a grid, line, staggered array, circle, arc, concentric pattern, perimeter or spiral. Counts are capped at 4,096 for browser performance; a count of one is valid.</li><li>Set contact dimensions and center-to-center pitch. Add another pattern, or place individual contacts. Click or box-select a subset; change its shape, dimensions, coordinates, exit side or metal layer in Selection.</li><li>Choose a connector-bank arrangement, trace width, clearance and metal-plane count. Compare the number of completed routes, total length and assumed trace resistance.</li><li>Inspect unresolved airwires and rule findings. Try more exit sides, a different pad order, narrower traces within your process rules, or a smaller array.</li><li>Save JSON to keep an editable design. Export individual metal planes, contact openings and substrate footprints for downstream review.</li></ol><p>Shift-click adds or removes contacts from a selection. Arrow keys nudge; shift-arrow moves ten grid intervals. Ctrl/Cmd-Z undoes. Scroll zooms around the pointer; space-drag pans. The Selection ID list and numeric fields provide an alternative to pointer editing. Designs save locally in the current browser.</p></section>
    <section id="contacts"><h2>Contact geometry and spatial sampling</h2><p>Circle, square, rectangle, ellipse and annular contacts offer different area, perimeter and packing relationships. Area is computed analytically: a circular contact has area πd²/4; a ring has area π(D² − d²)/4. A ring lead snaps onto the conductive annulus, rather than the central hole.</p><p>Ring and high-perimeter geometries appear in studies of platinum contacts and deep brain stimulation. Their effects depend on electrode material, surface structure, electrolyte and the modeled or measured stimulation conditions. The tool deliberately reports geometry without assigning a universal “best” shape or impedance. <a href="#s4">[4]</a> <a href="#s5">[5]</a> Material-dependent microelectrode scaling further motivates that separation. <a href="#s7">[7]</a></p><p>Grids make coordinate indexing simple; staggered layouts change neighborhood distances; lines sample along one direction; circles organize contacts around a center. These are geometric choices in this editor. Choosing pitch for a biological target also requires the target’s anatomy, signal bandwidth, recording depth and field model; this workspace has no tissue or signal-source model.</p><p>Geometric exposed area is distinct from electrochemically active surface area. A porous coating or roughness is not captured by the 2D outline. Contact rotation is independent of array rotation. Overlap checks use exact radial spacing for circular / ring outlines and conservative axis-aligned envelopes for other shapes; some rotated shapes can produce conservative warnings.</p></section>
    <section id="routing"><h2>Lead exits, pad order and independent channels</h2><p>Each electrode is a separate electrical net with a unique channel and pad ID. “Interleaved” describes where leads exit, not electrically joining electrodes together. The arrangement choices below are engineering heuristics implemented in this editor; they are not presented as configurations proven superior by the source papers.</p><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Arrangement</th><th>Reason to explore it</th><th>Tradeoff</th></tr></thead><tbody>{exits.map(row=><tr>{row.map(cell=><td>{cell}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody></table></div>
    <p>Position order places pads along their bank by contact position. Creation order preserves the channel sequence; reverse, column and row-serpentine orders let you explore alternative pin mappings. Pad order changes routing congestion even when contact positions stay fixed. A row-serpentine <em>pin order</em> is distinct from a serpentine <em>physical trace</em>.</p><p>The router uses a clearance-aware orthogonal search with conservative contact and pad envelopes. It routes outward contacts first, tries available planes for automatic assignments, and smooths corners only when the sampled route remains clear. Direct straight leads are checked rather than silently declared connected. Red dashed airwires have no completed conductive path.</p><p>It is a bounded heuristic, not an optimal autorouter: routing order can leave valid paths undiscovered. Large layouts use a coarser routing grid and a finite search budget. Unresolved contacts stay in the netlist with their status. Add planes, redistribute banks or adjust spacing before treating the layout as connected.</p></section>
    <section id="mechanics"><h2>Straight, rounded and serpentine leads</h2><p>Straight leads minimize centerline length for fixed endpoints, but can intersect unrelated contacts or traces. Orthogonal leads offer predictable routing corridors. Rounded leads replace corners with sampled quadratic curves; “corner setback” controls how far the curve starts from a corner, not a guaranteed circular bend radius.</p><p>Serpentine cable shapes can reduce stiffness and accommodate displacement. Sankar et al. measured higher compliance in their complete serpentine polyimide interconnect than in the compared straight design. The result depends on the tested cable geometry, thickness and loading; the paper does not establish that rounding any trace improves chronic tissue response. <a href="#s2">[2]</a></p><p>More recent work compares thin-film linear, serpentine and open-lattice ribbon cables with a-SiC / polyimide structure and Ti / Au traces. Bending tolerance and tensile extensibility are separate properties; the architecture around the traces matters. <a href="#s3">[3]</a></p><p>In this tool, the serpentine option adds a smooth oscillation to the longest eligible segment. The conformal-ribbon substrate option adds margin around the resulting paths. These are geometry concepts for exploring space and path length, without finite-element strain, fatigue, adhesion, encapsulation, tissue response or biocompatibility predictions. A metal wave embedded in a broad continuous substrate is not automatically a compliant cable.</p></section>
    <section id="fabrication"><h2>Photolithography and the multilayer stack</h2><p>A thin-film interface needs patterned conductor and insulating layers, exposed recording / stimulation windows and a substrate outline. Photosensitive-polyimide fabrication demonstrates how those layers can support varied neural electrode architectures. <a href="#s1">[1]</a> The editor therefore keeps metal, openings and substrate exports separate.</p><p>A 2026 high-channel-count μECoG platform reports four metal routing layers, 1,344 channels per layer, 2 µm minimum metal linewidth and 2.5 µm minimum spacing in its particular process. <a href="#s6">[6]</a> “Published 2026” sets those two rule thresholds; it does not reproduce the device, metal thicknesses, via technology, lithographic alignment or yield. The default 3 / 3 µm rules are illustrative user-editable values.</p><p>Increasing metal-plane count makes additional routing corridors available. A contact, trace and pad stay on one plane in this implementation. Cross-plane overlap is treated as electrically isolated, but there are no generated vias, interlayer dielectric geometry or process-specific access openings. The openings export is a nominal plan-view template, not a qualified multilayer opening sequence.</p><p>Gold / platinum conductor and polyimide / Parylene-C / SU-8 metadata let you label assumptions, rather than infer a process. A real mask set requires the chosen fabrication facility’s minimum widths, gaps, overlay allowances, alignment marks, etch / lift-off bias, pad finishes, passivation overlaps, minimum radii and release / packaging constraints. The tool’s preliminary checks cover only a subset of these.</p></section>
    <section id="exports"><h2>Dimensions, layers and export conventions</h2><div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Output</th><th>What it contains</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>Design JSON</td><td>Versioned editable contacts, routing and stack assumptions; current evaluated routes, pads and checks when available. Import recomputes routes rather than trusting stored paths.</td></tr><tr><td>Netlist CSV</td><td>Contact-to-pad mapping, channel, exit side, metal layer, route status, area, width, length and assumed trace resistance.</td></tr><tr><td>Review SVG</td><td>Colored metal planes and substrate footprint. Cross-plane shapes overlap visually but remain grouped by layer.</td></tr><tr><td>Metal SVG</td><td>One selected metal plane in black, with contacts, completed traces and connector pads. Unresolved airwires are excluded. Conflicting direct leads remain visible; consult the checks before use.</td></tr><tr><td>Contact openings SVG</td><td>Nominal plan-view contact and pad windows. Rings retain their central hole. Process-specific overlap and layer-access corrections are not included.</td></tr><tr><td>Substrate SVG</td><td>Rounded bounding envelope or conformal ribbons with expanded contact / pad regions. This is a concept footprint, without mechanical or release-process qualification.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div><p>Coordinates are in micrometers. SVG viewBox coordinates use those same units, with outer dimensions expressed in millimeters to preserve physical size. Rounded and serpentine centerlines export as sampled polylines. Trace thickness is not modeled; substrate thickness is metadata. A minimum visible line width on the screen helps inspection at low zoom; SVG retains the requested physical trace width.</p><p>Estimated trace resistance is R = (length / width) × R<sub>sheet</sub>. The default 0.2 Ω/□ is an illustrative user assumption and does not change when the material label changes. This excludes electrode / electrolyte impedance, connector resistance, parasitic capacitance, leakage and frequency-dependent behavior.</p><p>This version supplies editable geometry and a netlist for downstream simulations. It does not yet inject a custom design into the cortical recording lab or the peripheral-nerve sandbox. Keep the JSON to retain all layout and routing assumptions.</p></section>
    <section id="sources"><h2>Primary research</h2><p class="reviewed">Source review: 5 October 2026. The design rules above distinguish measured paper results from the editor’s own routing choices.</p><div class="sources">{papers.map((p,i)=><div class="source" id={p.id}><span class="source-meta">[{i+1}] {p.authors} · {p.year}</span><h3><a href={p.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{p.title} ↗</a></h3><p>{p.takeaway}</p></div>)}</div></section>
    <a class="back" href="/simulations/interface-designer/">Open the designer →</a>
  </article>
</Layout>
